WEBVTT

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.720
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:11.720
The next topic in our series of mini lectures is subtraction.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:34.600
This time around we are going to look into subtraction in context of sign to complement numbers.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:49.300
So this is the theoretical explanation of how we are going to look at subtraction.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:57.300
If you have a number A, and you kind of do not care if that number is positive or negative,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:21.060
and then you have a positive number B, in order to subtract, what we want to do is to take this first number, A,

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:32.850
And instead of subtracting, we want to add, but what we want to add is actually minus b.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:47.340
Let's look at the similar example, but in this case, b is a negative number.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:02:00.760
It's annotated as minus b.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:08.760
So in this example, we also want to utilize, actually, addition.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:28.170
What is minus minus b? Plus b. Now, let's think about this in terms of sign numbers,

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:51.020
binary sign numbers. If we look into minus b, that one, it is a 2's complement of the

00:02:51.020 --> 00:03:04.360
number plus b. Now, I hope this is not going to be confusing. What if we have

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:36.630
minus, minus B, that adds something plus B. Well, it is a two's complement of, so this

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:53.820
is two's complement of plus B. This one is two's complement of minus B. And we might

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:59.760
be using this and you'll see how when we start practicing. We are going to go

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:07.020
through one example and then we are going to do more practice in our life

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:14.120
one-on-one, not one-on-one, life synchronous lectures. So what if we want

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:25.120
to do decimal 4 minus decimal 2. Let's do it this way. We're going to take 4

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:35.180
in decimal and we are going to convert it to binary then we are going to do

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:43.140
minus two and instead of performing subtraction we are going to perform

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:51.460
addition actually so two to the power of zero two to the power of one two to the

00:04:51.460 --> 00:05:09.240
power of 2, and a little bit lopsided. 0, so it's 4-bit representation. How do we get

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:49.180
minus 2. Start with plus 2, 0, 0, 1, 0. This is in binary. Flip it. 1, 0, 1, 1. Add 1. 1

00:05:49.180 --> 00:06:01.290
1 and 1 is 0, carry 1. These 3 are 1, carry 0.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:11.290
3 are 1, carry 0. These 3 are 1, carry 0.

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:20.290
So now, finally, we have minus 2 in 2's complement, 1, 1, 1, 0.

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:23.600
What are we noting?

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:28.600
The most significant bit is 1 that designates that this is negative.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:32.600
The most significant bit here is 0. It designates that this is positive.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:45.600
Okay, so let's do plus 4 plus minus 2 plus 4 0 1 0 0 minus 2 1 1 1 0

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.800
We might have some carries over here

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:57.800
0 and 0 is 0 carry 0

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:02.800
3 of them together are 1 carry 0

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:15.800
three of them together is zero, carry one. Two of them, three of them together is zero, another carry one.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:22.210
So let's look at the result. Two things that we need to look at the result.

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:33.210
One is we need to look at both four bits and the other thing that we learned in the previous module we need to look at those two most significant bit carries.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:51.730
Two conclusions. First conclusion. These two are the same. And we know from there, there is no overflow.

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:59.730
So we know then that this is actually correct result. Now let's look at the result. That's what we learned last week.

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:08.100
So two things again that we have to learn. First thing, we have to observe the most significant bit.

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:12.100
By observing most significant bit we are going to learn the sign.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:17.100
So sign is going to be plus because most significant bit is one.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:23.100
Then we need to look at all the other bits and find the absolute value.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:33.100
Other bits are going to give us 2, so plus 2 decimal,

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:40.920
which actually is a correct answer in a small way.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:47.920
We will be practicing all of this. Please don't hesitate to pose any questions if you have.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:49.920
Thank you for watching.

